{"availability":"available","full":"Spleefy167/frida-orbit-lab","name":"frida-orbit-lab","description":"SpacePeng Trainer Frida Scripts and Game Hacking Examples 2026","category":"Developer tools","language":"HTML","stars":53,"forks":0,"created":"2026-09-21T22:27:40Z","updated":"2026-10-09T21:32:50Z","branch":"main","homepage":null,"topics":["androguard","android","burp-suite","course","dalvik-opcodes","dex2jar","frida","game","ghidra","hacking","instrumentation-toolkit","jadx","objection","reverse-engineer","shooter","smali","space","spacepeng","trainer","wireshark"],"demo":"https://spleefy167.github.io/frida-orbit-lab/","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T22:06:51.831Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-09T22:06:51.831Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-09T22:06:51.831Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T22:06:51.831Z","stars":53,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["An open, curious-minded toolkit for people who like to peer beneath the hood of small 2D arcade titles and learn how memory, timing, and game state actually behave in the wild. Where the original SpacePeng trainer experiment treated a single penguin-flinging game as a lab rat, OrbitKit generalizes the entire idea into a reusable runtime instrumentation playground: a curated set of examples, helper utilities, and documented experiments that demonstrate how dynamic code instrumentation, hooking, and state inspection can be applied to lightweight games, prototypes, and educational software.","Think of it less as a \"trainer\" and more as a microscope for toy software — a way to watch values move, trace function calls, and build a mental model of how a game ticks. Whether you are a reverse-engineering hobbyist, a computer-science student studying process memory, or a solo developer wanting to understand your own build from the outside, OrbitKit gives you a well-lit sandbox to play in.","The repository is deliberately structured as a teaching artifact. Every example is annotated, every hook is explained, and every module answers a specific \"why\" rather than just a \"how.\""],"features":["Study working instrumentation examples without hunting through forum threads.","Compare multiple approaches (scripted instrumentation vs. native modules) side by side.","Reuse small helper utilities in their own experiments.","Understand process memory in a low-stakes, low-risk context.","🧩 Modular Example Set — each example is self-contained and can be studied independently."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Spleefy167/frida-orbit-lab/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"63aa4e8b45db3ef344550329d2699284ef3246d6d289e71fcd9a664fff7d0f8f","extractedAt":"2026-10-09T18:01:13.113Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T22:06:51.920Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"An open, curious-minded toolkit for people who like to peer beneath the hood of small 2D arcade titles and learn how memory, timing, and game state actually behave in the wild. Where the original SpacePeng trainer experiment treated a single penguin-flinging game as a lab rat, OrbitKit generalizes the entire idea into a reusable runtime instrumentation playground: a curated set of examples, helper utilities, and documented experiments that demonstrate how dynamic code instrumentation, hooking, and state inspection can be applied to lightweight games, prototypes, and educational software.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Spleefy167/frida-orbit-lab/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"63aa4e8b45db3ef344550329d2699284ef3246d6d289e71fcd9a664fff7d0f8f","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Spleefy167/frida-orbit-lab/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-09T22:06:51.921Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2026-10-09T21:32:50Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Spleefy167/frida-orbit-lab/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-09T22:06:51.922Z","hash":"63aa4e8b45db3ef344550329d2699284ef3246d6d289e71f